1.1 Introduction to permutations
================================

The ‘permutations’ package adds permutation groups to Maxima, built on
Robert Smith's cl-permutation
(https://github.com/stylewarning/cl-permutation).  Maxima's
‘combinatorics’ package works on single permutations.  This package adds
groups: order and membership by Schreier-Sims, subgroups and cosets,
homomorphisms and quotients, conjugacy classes, Sylow subgroups, series,
actions and counting.

*Requirements:* SBCL (Steel Bank Common Lisp).  ‘mxpm install’ fetches
cl-permutation through Quicklisp.

* Menu:

* Quick start::
* Permutations::
* Conventions::
* Limits::


File: permutations.info,  Node: Quick start,  Next: Permutations,  Up: Introduction to permutations

1.1.1 Quick start
-----------------

(%i1) S4 : pgrp_symmetric(4);
(%o1) pgrp(degree 4, 2 generators)
(%i2) A4 : pgrp_alternating(4)$
(%i3) [pgrp_order(S4), pgrp_order(A4), pgrp_normal_p(A4, S4)];
(%o3) [24, 12, true]
(%i4) r : pgrp_from_cycles([[1, 2, 3, 4]])$
(%i5) s : pgrp_from_cycles([[2, 4]])$
(%i6) [pgrp_compose(r, s), pgrp_compose(s, r)];
(%o6) [(1 2)(3 4), (1 4)(2 3)]
(%i7) pgrp_member_p(A4, r);
(%o7) false
(%i8) pgrp_elements(pgrp_centre(pgrp_dihedral(4)));
(%o8) [(), (1 3)(2 4)]


File: permutations.info,  Node: Permutations,  Next: Conventions,  Prev: Quick start,  Up: Introduction to permutations

1.1.2 Permutations
------------------

Results that are permutations come back as ‘pgrp_perm’ values, shown in
cycle notation, with ‘()’ for the identity.  Every function also accepts
a list of images, the form the ‘combinatorics’ package uses: ‘[2,1,3]’
maps 1 to 2, 2 to 1 and 3 to 3.  A permutation value keeps its degree,
so (1 2) in S_4 has cycle type [2, 1, 1].  Equal permutations of the
same degree are the same value, so ‘=’, ‘member’, ‘unique’ and ‘setify’
work on them; ‘pgrp_eq’ compares across degrees.  Set ‘pgrp_perm_output
: false’ to get lists of images back.


File: permutations.info,  Node: Conventions,  Next: Limits,  Prev: Permutations,  Up: Introduction to permutations

1.1.3 Conventions
-----------------

These follow Dummit and Foote, _Abstract Algebra_.

   • Composition reads right to left: ‘pgrp_compose(s, t)’ is s∘t, which
     applies ‘t’ first.  The ‘combinatorics’ package's ‘permult’ reads
     the other way.
   • D_2n is the dihedral group of order 2n, generated by r = (1 2 ...
     n) and s, the reflection fixing vertex 1.
   • Conjugates are g x g^-1, and commutators are [x, y] = x^-1 y^-1 x
     y.
   • Actions are on the left, and ‘pgrp_quotient’ uses left cosets.


File: permutations.info,  Node: Limits,  Prev: Conventions,  Up: Introduction to permutations

1.1.4 Limits
------------

Most structure functions list a group's elements, so they refuse groups
with more than ‘pgrp_max_elements’ elements (100000 by default).
‘pgrp_order’, ‘pgrp_member_p’, ‘pgrp_orbits’ and ‘pgrp_stabiliser_chain’
don't list elements and work on groups of any size, such as the Rubik's
cube group of order 43252003274489856000.


