1.7 Actions and counting
========================

Any finite action gives a permutation group: number the points 1, ..., n
and record where each generator sends them.  Burnside's lemma and the
cycle index then count colourings up to symmetry.

* Menu:

* Function pgrp_from_action gens points act::
* Function pgrp_action_perm g points act::
* Function pgrp_orbit G x::
* Function pgrp_stabiliser G x::
* Function pgrp_count_orbits G k::
* Function pgrp_cycle_index G::
* Function pgrp_colouring_orbits G k::
* Function pgrp_regular G::


File: permutations.info,  Node: Function pgrp_from_action gens points act,  Next: Function pgrp_action_perm g points act,  Up: Actions and counting

 -- Function: pgrp_from_action (GENS, POINTS, ACT)
     Returns the permutation group that ‘gens’ induce on the list
     ‘points’ through ‘act(g, x)’.

     Point ‘i’ stands for ‘points[i]’.  ‘gens’ can be matrices,
     permutations or anything ‘act’ accepts, and ‘act(g, x)’ must return
     an element of ‘points’.  In the example, GL_2(F_2) acts on the
     three nonzero vectors of F_2^2 as S_3.

* Menu:

     * Examples: Examples 39.


File: permutations.info,  Node: Examples 39,  Up: Actions and counting

1.7.1 Examples
--------------

(%i1) X : [[0, 1], [1, 0], [1, 1]]$
(%i2) act : lambda([m, v], map(lambda([t], mod(t, 2)), list_matrix_entries(m . transpose(matrix(v)))))$
(%i3) G : pgrp_from_action([matrix([1, 1], [0, 1]), matrix([0, 1], [1, 0])], X, act)$
(%i4) [pgrp_order(G), pgrp_isomorphic_p(G, pgrp_symmetric(3))];
(%o4) [6, true]

