File: permutations.info,  Node: Function pgrp_count_orbits G k,  Next: Function pgrp_cycle_index G,  Prev: Function pgrp_stabiliser G x,  Up: Actions and counting

 -- Function: pgrp_count_orbits (G, K)
     Counts the colourings of the points of ‘G’ with ‘k’ colours,
     counting two as the same when an element of ‘G’ turns one into the
     other.

     This is Burnside's lemma: the average over ‘G’ of k^c(g), where
     c(g) is the number of cycles of g, fixed points included.  ‘k’ may
     be a symbol, which gives a polynomial.  The example colours the
     faces of a cube, labelled 1 up, 2 down, 3 front, 4 right, 5 back, 6
     left.

* Menu:

     * Examples: Examples 43.


File: permutations.info,  Node: Examples 43,  Next: Examples 44,  Prev: Examples 42,  Up: Examples 39

1.7.1.4 Examples
................

(%i1) cube : pgrp(pgrp_from_cycles([[3, 4, 5, 6]]), pgrp_from_cycles([[1, 4, 2, 6]]))$
(%i2) display2d : false$
(%i3) [pgrp_count_orbits(cube, 3), expand(pgrp_count_orbits(cube, k))];
(%o3) [57,k^6/24+k^4/8+k^3/2+k^2/3]

See also: ‘pgrp_cycle_index’, ‘pgrp_colouring_orbits’


File: permutations.info,  Node: Function pgrp_cycle_index G,  Next: Function pgrp_colouring_orbits G k,  Prev: Function pgrp_count_orbits G k,  Up: Actions and counting

 -- Function: pgrp_cycle_index (G)
     Returns the cycle index of ‘G’ as a polynomial in ‘x[1]’, ‘x[2]’,
     ...

     Calling forms:

        • ‘pgrp_cycle_index(G)’: in ‘x[1]’, ‘x[2]’, ...
        • ‘pgrp_cycle_index(G, y)’: in ‘y[1]’, ‘y[2]’, ...

     It averages x[1]^m_1 x[2]^m_2 ... over ‘G’, where m_j is the number
     of j-cycles.  Substituting x[j] = r^j + b^j counts colourings by
     how often each colour is used (Pólya's theorem).

* Menu:

     * Examples: Examples 44.


File: permutations.info,  Node: Examples 44,  Next: Examples 45,  Prev: Examples 43,  Up: Examples 39

1.7.1.5 Examples
................

(%i1) display2d : false$
(%i2) Z : pgrp_cycle_index(pgrp_cyclic(4));
(%o2) (2*x[4]+x[2]^2+x[1]^4)/4
(%i3) expand(subst(makelist(x[j] = r^j + b^j, j, 1, 4), Z));
(%o3) r^4+b*r^3+2*b^2*r^2+b^3*r+b^4

See also: ‘pgrp_count_orbits’


File: permutations.info,  Node: Function pgrp_colouring_orbits G k,  Next: Function pgrp_regular G,  Prev: Function pgrp_cycle_index G,  Up: Actions and counting

 -- Function: pgrp_colouring_orbits (G, K)
     Returns one colouring from each orbit of ‘G’ on the colourings of
     its points with colours 1, ..., ‘k’.

     A colouring is a list whose entry ‘i’ is the colour of point ‘i’;
     it is not a permutation.  Each colouring returned is the least in
     its orbit, and there are ‘pgrp_count_orbits(G, k)’ of them.  ‘G’
     moves a colouring by carrying the colour at point i to point g(i).

* Menu:

     * Examples: Examples 45.


File: permutations.info,  Node: Examples 45,  Next: Examples 46,  Prev: Examples 44,  Up: Examples 39

1.7.1.6 Examples
................

(%i1) pgrp_colouring_orbits(pgrp_dihedral(4), 2);
(%o1) [[1, 1, 1, 1], [1, 1, 1, 2], [1, 1, 2, 2], [1, 2, 1, 2], [1, 2, 2, 2], [2, 2, 2, 2]]

See also: ‘pgrp_count_orbits’


File: permutations.info,  Node: Function pgrp_regular G,  Prev: Function pgrp_colouring_orbits G k,  Up: Actions and counting

